/*Why is count = 4?

Because do...while executes the body first, then checks the condition.

Even when i becomes 0, the loop body runs one last time before checking 0 > 0.

Interview Answer

If the interviewer asks, "What is the output and why?", you can say:

Output: 4 -1

Reason: A do...while loop executes its body before checking the condition. The condition uses i--, which is a post-decrement operator. It compares the current value of i with 0 first, then decrements i. On the last iteration, the body executes when i is 0, count becomes 4, the condition 0 > 0 evaluates to false, and then i is decremented to -1, ending the loop.
